<DocsLayout title="VLO Guide · Overview" heading="Build with VLO." eyebrow="Overview"
    lede="The complete guide to building, extending and shipping VLO projects. Five chapters cover the templating engine, the backend, media and mail, and the day-to-day workflow.">
    <section class="intro-card">
        <div class="intro-icon">V</div>
        <div>
            <strong>VLO is a component-first framework.</strong>
            <p>Build interfaces from small reusable components instead of repeating markup and styles throughout the project. Every page in this guide is itself built with VLO components.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <Callout variant="tip" dismissible title="What is new in this guide">
        The guide is now split into five chapters, documents multi-database support and schema dumps, and ships two example modules (<code>callout</code> and <code>kbd</code>) so the module system can be verified end to end.
    </Callout>

    <section class="doc-section" id="principles">
        <div class="section-number">01</div>
        <h2>Core Principles</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">Every VLO project should follow a small set of principles. These principles matter more than any individual implementation.</p>
        <div class="principles-grid">
            <article><div class="principle-icon">◈</div><h3>Reusable</h3><p>Build components once and reuse them everywhere they are needed.</p></article>
            <article><div class="principle-icon">⌘</div><h3>Simple</h3><p>Prefer straightforward implementations over unnecessary abstraction.</p></article>
            <article><div class="principle-icon">◇</div><h3>Consistent</h3><p>Similar problems should have similar solutions throughout the project.</p></article>
            <article><div class="principle-icon">✦</div><h3>Maintainable</h3><p>Write code that another developer can understand and modify quickly.</p></article>
        </div>
        <div class="rule-card">
            <strong>Golden Rule</strong>
            <p>If you have written the same UI pattern more than once, consider turning it into a component.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section" id="chapters">
        <div class="section-number">02</div>
        <h2>The Guide</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">Each chapter is a self-contained reference. Read them in order the first time, then jump straight to the section you need.</p>
        <div class="chapter-grid">
            <a class="chapter-card" href="/docs/templating">
                <span class="chapter-no">CHAPTER 02</span>
                <strong>Templating</strong>
                <p>Interpolation, conditionals, loops, modifiers, directives, data sources, slots and AJAX navigation.</p>
                <span class="chapter-go">Open chapter →</span>
            </a>
            <a class="chapter-card" href="/docs/backend">
                <span class="chapter-no">CHAPTER 03</span>
                <strong>Backend</strong>
                <p>Authentication, page guards, API security, SQL-backed API definitions, pagination, multi-database support and modules.</p>
                <span class="chapter-go">Open chapter →</span>
            </a>
            <a class="chapter-card" href="/docs/media">
                <span class="chapter-no">CHAPTER 04</span>
                <strong>Media &amp; Mail</strong>
                <p>Flash messages, multi-file uploads, orphaned file cleanup, the image gallery and transactional email templates.</p>
                <span class="chapter-go">Open chapter →</span>
            </a>
            <a class="chapter-card" href="/docs/workflow">
                <span class="chapter-no">CHAPTER 05</span>
                <strong>Workflow</strong>
                <p>Pages, layouts, styling, branding, performance, accessibility, security, CLI commands and the release checklist.</p>
                <span class="chapter-go">Open chapter →</span>
            </a>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section" id="structure">
        <div class="section-number">03</div>
        <h2>Project Structure</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">Keep project files organized by responsibility. Avoid putting unrelated functionality into one large file.</p>
        <div class="code-card">
            <div class="code-header"><span>Recommended structure</span><span>VLO</span></div>
            <pre><code>project/
├── pages/
│   ├── index.vlo
│   ├── docs.vlo
│   ├── docs/
│   │   ├── templating.vlo
│   │   ├── backend.vlo
│   │   ├── media.vlo
│   │   └── workflow.vlo
│   ├── login.vlo
│   ├── api/
│   │   └── api.vlo
│   └── admin/
│       ├── users.vlo
│       └── dashboard.vlo
├── components/
│   ├── Alert.vlo
│   ├── Button.vlo
│   ├── Card.vlo
│   └── ...
├── layouts/
│   ├── BaseLayout.vlo
│   └── DocsLayout.vlo
├── modules/
│   ├── charting/
│   │   ├── module.json
│   │   ├── components/
│   │   ├── styles/
│   │   └── scripts/
│   ├── callout/
│   └── kbd/
├── email_templates/
│   └── welcome.html
├── schema.sql
├── schema-pg.sql
├── schema-mysql.sql
└── .env</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="info-grid">
            <div><strong>pages/</strong><p>Application routes and complete pages. Subdirectories map to URL paths.</p></div>
            <div><strong>components/</strong><p>Reusable UI building blocks available to every page.</p></div>
            <div><strong>layouts/</strong><p>Shared page structures with automatic hierarchical resolution.</p></div>
            <div><strong>modules/</strong><p>Self-contained packages of components, styles, scripts and data handlers.</p></div>
            <div><strong>email_templates/</strong><p>HTML email templates with subject extraction and modifier support.</p></div>
            <div><strong>schema*.sql</strong><p>Canonical SQLite schema plus generated PostgreSQL and MySQL dumps.</p></div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section" id="vlo-files">
        <div class="section-number">04</div>
        <h2>.vlo Files</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">A VLO file has one clear responsibility. Keep markup, styling and behavior close to the component they belong to.</p>
        <div class="code-card">
            <div class="code-header"><span>components/ExampleComponent.vlo</span><span>.vlo</span></div>
            <pre><code>&lt;div class="example"&gt;
    &lt;h2&gt;{{title}}&lt;/h2&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;{{description}}&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;style&gt;
.example { padding: 20px; }
&lt;/style&gt;</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p class="component-note">A component may contain markup, a scoped <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> block and an optional <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> block. Styles are collected and injected into the page head once, no matter how many instances the page renders.</p>
        <div class="do-grid">
            <div class="do-card good">
                <div class="do-title"><span>✓</span> Do</div>
                <ul><li>Keep components focused.</li><li>Use meaningful names.</li><li>Keep styles scoped.</li><li>Document unusual behavior.</li></ul>
            </div>
            <div class="do-card avoid">
                <div class="do-title"><span>×</span> Avoid</div>
                <ul><li>Huge monolithic components.</li><li>Duplicated markup.</li><li>Unused props.</li><li>Unexplained magic values.</li></ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section" id="components">
        <div class="section-number">05</div>
        <h2>Components</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">Components are the foundation of VLO. A component represents a reusable piece of interface or behavior.</p>
        <h3>Component catalog</h3>
        <p class="component-note">These components ship with the project. Every one accepts props, supports scoped styles and can be extended.</p>
        <div class="principles-grid">
            <article><div class="component-glyph">A</div><h3>Alert</h3><p>Inline status messages for success, error, warning and info states.</p></article>
            <article><div class="component-glyph">B</div><h3>Badge</h3><p>Compact label pills for statuses, tags and metadata.</p></article>
            <article><div class="component-glyph">Bt</div><h3>Button</h3><p>Primary, secondary, ghost and danger actions with href support.</p></article>
            <article><div class="component-glyph">C</div><h3>Card</h3><p>Titled content container with description and default slot.</p></article>
            <article><div class="component-glyph">Fi</div><h3>FileInput</h3><p>Styled file picker for multipart uploads with a custom button label.</p></article>
            <article><div class="component-glyph">G</div><h3>ImageGallery</h3><p>Pure CSS zoomable gallery with thumbnails and native download support.</p></article>
            <article><div class="component-glyph">I</div><h3>Input</h3><p>Form field with label, placeholder and validation attributes.</p></article>
            <article><div class="component-glyph">L</div><h3>Link</h3><p>Consistently styled anchor with variants and active state.</p></article>
            <article><div class="component-glyph">M</div><h3>Modal</h3><p>Dialog overlay for edit forms and delete confirmations.</p></article>
            <article><div class="component-glyph">N</div><h3>Navbar</h3><p>Site navigation with variants, sizes, shadows, sticky and mobile modes.</p></article>
            <article><div class="component-glyph">P</div><h3>Pagination</h3><p>Prev/Next navigation with page counter and clean URLs.</p></article>
            <article><div class="component-glyph">S</div><h3>Sidebar</h3><p>Vertical navigation panel for dashboards and docs sections.</p></article>
            <article><div class="component-glyph">Sc</div><h3>SlotCard</h3><p>Card with named slots for header, body and footer content.</p></article>
            <article><div class="component-glyph">T</div><h3>Toast</h3><p>Auto-dismissing notification for API action feedback.</p></article>
        </div>
        <h3>Module components</h3>
        <p class="component-note">Modules add components without touching the core. These come from the bundled example modules and are documented in <a href="/docs/backend#modules">chapter 03</a>.</p>
        <div class="principles-grid">
            <article><div class="component-glyph">Ca</div><h3>Callout</h3><p>Dismissible note, tip, warning and danger boxes from the <code>callout</code> module.</p></article>
            <article><div class="component-glyph">K</div><h3>Kbd</h3><p>Keyboard badge for shortcuts from the <code>kbd</code> module.</p></article>
        </div>
        <h3>Component naming</h3>
        <div class="naming-table">
            <div class="naming-row header"><span>Type</span><span>Recommended</span><span>Avoid</span></div>
            <div class="naming-row"><span>Button</span><span>Button.vlo</span><span>button.vlo</span></div>
            <div class="naming-row"><span>Navigation</span><span>Navbar.vlo</span><span>navthing.vlo</span></div>
            <div class="naming-row"><span>Form</span><span>Input.vlo</span><span>inputThing.vlo</span></div>
        </div>
        <h3>Component responsibilities</h3>
        <div class="number-list">
            <div><b>01</b><p>A component owns its own presentation.</p></div>
            <div><b>02</b><p>Props control configurable behavior.</p></div>
            <div><b>03</b><p>Children and named slots provide flexible content.</p></div>
            <div><b>04</b><p>Avoid coupling components to specific pages.</p></div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section" id="props">
        <div class="section-number">06</div>
        <h2>Props</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">Props are the primary way to customize reusable VLO components.</p>
        <div class="code-card">
            <div class="code-header"><span>Props example</span><span>VLO</span></div>
            <pre><code>&lt;Button text="Save Changes" href="/save" variant="primary" /&gt;

&lt;Callout variant="warning" title="Unsaved changes" dismissible&gt;
    Your edits are stored automatically every 350 ms.
&lt;/Callout&gt;</code></pre>
        </div>
        <h3>Prop rules</h3>
        <div class="rules-list">
            <div><span>01</span><p>Use clear, descriptive prop names.</p></div>
            <div><span>02</span><p>Provide sensible defaults when possible.</p></div>
            <div><span>03</span><p>Avoid props that only make sense for one page.</p></div>
            <div><span>04</span><p>Keep variant names predictable.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="rule-card">
            <strong>Good example</strong>
            <p><code>variant="primary"</code>, <code>variant="secondary"</code>, <code>variant="ghost"</code></p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section" id="features">
        <div class="section-number">07</div>
        <h2>Feature Index</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">Every framework feature, and where to read about it.</p>
        <div class="feature-table">
            <div class="feature-row header"><span>Feature</span><span>Chapter</span><span>What it covers</span></div>
            <div class="feature-row"><span>Interpolation</span><a href="/docs/templating#interpolation">02</a><span>Variables, dot notation and attribute-safe output</span></div>
            <div class="feature-row"><span>Conditionals</span><a href="/docs/templating#conditionals">02</a><span><code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, comparison operators and truthy checks</span></div>
            <div class="feature-row"><span>Loops</span><a href="/docs/templating#loops">02</a><span>Array iteration, JSON columns and empty states</span></div>
            <div class="feature-row"><span>Modifiers</span><a href="/docs/templating#modifiers">02</a><span>Text, number, array, AJAX and live-update pipes</span></div>
            <div class="feature-row"><span>Directives</span><a href="/docs/templating#directives">02</a><span><code>v-post</code>, <code>v-put</code>, <code>v-delete</code>, <code>v-auto</code>, modals</span></div>
            <div class="feature-row"><span>Data sources</span><a href="/docs/templating#data-sources">02</a><span>Server-side API binding and AJAX navigation</span></div>
            <div class="feature-row"><span>Slots</span><a href="/docs/templating#slots">02</a><span>Default and named slots in custom components</span></div>
            <div class="feature-row"><span>Authentication</span><a href="/docs/backend#authentication">03</a><span>Registration, verification, reset, sessions, CSRF</span></div>
            <div class="feature-row"><span>Page guards</span><a href="/docs/backend#page-guards">03</a><span><code>auth</code>, <code>guest</code> and <code>roles</code> attributes</span></div>
            <div class="feature-row"><span>API security</span><a href="/docs/backend#api-security">03</a><span>Session enforcement and public route allowlist</span></div>
            <div class="feature-row"><span>API definitions</span><a href="/docs/backend#api-database">03</a><span>SQL-backed REST endpoints in <code>api.vlo</code></span></div>
            <div class="feature-row"><span>Pagination</span><a href="/docs/backend#pagination">03</a><span>Automatic counting and the Pagination component</span></div>
            <div class="feature-row"><span>Multi-database</span><a href="/docs/backend#databases">03</a><span>SQLite, PostgreSQL and MySQL with schema dumps</span></div>
            <div class="feature-row"><span>Modules</span><a href="/docs/backend#modules">03</a><span>Manifests, discovery, handler pipeline, asset injection</span></div>
            <div class="feature-row"><span>Flash messages</span><a href="/docs/media#flash">04</a><span>One-time cookie-based feedback with Toast</span></div>
            <div class="feature-row"><span>File uploads</span><a href="/docs/media#uploads">04</a><span>Multi-file JSON aggregation and orphan cleanup</span></div>
            <div class="feature-row"><span>Image gallery</span><a href="/docs/media#gallery">04</a><span>Zero-JS zoom, thumbnails and downloads</span></div>
            <div class="feature-row"><span>Email</span><a href="/docs/media#email">04</a><span>Templates, subjects, modifiers and mail drivers</span></div>
            <div class="feature-row"><span>Pages &amp; layouts</span><a href="/docs/workflow#pages">05</a><span>Routing, hierarchy and automatic layout resolution</span></div>
            <div class="feature-row"><span>Styling &amp; branding</span><a href="/docs/workflow#styling">05</a><span>Design tokens, navbar variants and visual identity</span></div>
            <div class="feature-row"><span>Performance</span><a href="/docs/workflow#performance">05</a><span>Caching, background I/O and event delegation</span></div>
            <div class="feature-row"><span>Accessibility</span><a href="/docs/workflow#accessibility">05</a><span>Keyboard, contrast, labels and semantics</span></div>
            <div class="feature-row"><span>Security</span><a href="/docs/workflow#security">05</a><span>Secrets, input validation and dependency control</span></div>
            <div class="feature-row"><span>Features &amp; CLI</span><a href="/docs/workflow#workflow">05</a><span><code>VLO_FEATURES</code>, dev, build, serve, schema</span></div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="final-card">
        <div class="final-mark">VLO</div>
        <h2>Build once. Reuse everywhere.</h2>
        <p>VLO is more than a collection of components. It is a shared language for building consistent, maintainable interfaces.</p>
        <div class="final-links">
            <a href="/docs/templating">Start with templating →</a>
            <a href="/components">Explore components →</a>
        </div>
    </section>
</DocsLayout>
